WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:29.960
This test will help us get ready for the future test during the final test.

00:30.960 --> 00:43.960
We will be doing further mobility testing for Viper to understand the rover's performance in terms of how well the rover can traverse simulated lunar soil, how well it can overcome obstacles like rocks.

00:43.960 --> 00:50.960
And slope is the ideal location for this type of mobility test. It's a world-class facility.

00:50.960 --> 00:59.960
We have a really well-characterized soil. We have unique capability that it's indoors and we can control the environment very well.

00:59.960 --> 01:09.960
The tilt bed on the slope itself is very well controlled. We've recently added software upgrades and advanced our optical tracking system quite a bit.

01:09.960 --> 01:19.960
We install the 16-camera infrared tracking system, which allow us in the software to identify individual pieces of the vehicle, which we can track in real time.

01:19.960 --> 01:32.960
It has been challenging to work through this pandemic, but this pandemic has also brought opportunities to look and understand and learn how we can keep highly performing and engaging teams.

01:32.960 --> 01:43.960
We encountered procurement delays, COVID delays, snow delays, and we still pulled it off and made it happen, and it was a huge team effort to get here.

01:43.960 --> 01:56.960
Viper is the rover of many firsts. The first NASA rover to map water resources, and it's the first rover to wear a headlight.

01:56.960 --> 02:05.960
For many, many years, I was dreaming about joining NASA and sending big rovers to Mars and other places in the solar system.

02:06.960 --> 02:14.960
So that dream came through last year when I joined NASA Glenn to lead and manage projects for future missions to Mars.

02:14.960 --> 02:18.960
And I love Mars, but I also love the Moon and the Artemis missions.

